<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><gpx xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" version="1.0" creator="Groundspeak, Inc.All Rights Reserved.http://www.groundspeak.com" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/0 http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/0/gpx.xsd http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1 http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1/cache.xsd" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/0"><name>Cache Listing Generated from Geocaching.com</name><desc>This is an individual cache generated from Geocaching.com</desc><email>contact@geocaching.com</email><url>http://www.geocaching.com</url><urlname>Geocaching - High Tech Treasure Hunting</urlname><time>2026-03-02T05:14:51.1188439Z</time><keywords>cache, geocache</keywords><wpt lat="39.430783" lon="-84.4715"><time>2014-07-09T00:00:00</time><name>GC55YCE</name><desc>Doc's Adventures #2 by DocSnappy</desc><url>https://www.geocaching.com/geocache/GC55YCE</url><urlname>Doc's Adventures #2</urlname><sym>Geocache</sym><type>Geocache|Traditional Cache</type><groundspeak:cache id="4383695" available="true" archived="false"><groundspeak:name>Doc's Adventures #2</groundspeak:name><groundspeak:placed_by>DocSnappy</groundspeak:placed_by><groundspeak:owner id="603377">docsnappy</groundspeak:owner><groundspeak:type>Traditional Cache</groundspeak:type><groundspeak:container>Regular</groundspeak:container><groundspeak:attributes><groundspeak:attribute id="8" inc="1">Scenic view</groundspeak:attribute><groundspeak:attribute id="11" inc="1">May require wading</groundspeak:attribute><groundspeak:attribute id="4" inc="1">Boat required</groundspeak:attribute></groundspeak:attributes><groundspeak:difficulty>2</groundspeak:difficulty><groundspeak:terrain>5</groundspeak:terrain><groundspeak:country>United States</groundspeak:country><groundspeak:state>Ohio</groundspeak:state><groundspeak:short_description>Dennis and Doc have been out on another adventure, exploring another island in the Great Miami River. This time, you're definitely going to need a boat!
</groundspeak:short_description><groundspeak:long_description>&lt;p&gt;Doc's Adventures #2 takes us to an island in the Great Miami River, just upstream from the Wayne Madison Rd Bridge. Unlike DA1, there's no land access to this cache, by trespass or otherwise. &lt;b&gt;While it may be tempting to look at the closest land access and consider swimming, DON"T. The currents passing the island are swift on both sides and would be dangerous to a swimmer!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To access this cache, we suggest one of a couple possible adventures (in no particular order):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1 - Put in to the GMR where SR 73 crosses, east of Trenton. Enjoy the run down the river, stop for the cache, and then take out either at the old Wayne Madison Bridge, Rentschler Forest, or perhaps even run all the way down to Comb's Park. If you go that far, you can pick up &lt;a href="http://www.geocaching.com/geocache/GC4FC6Z_docs-adventures-1&amp;quot;"&gt;Doc's Adventures #1&lt;/a&gt; on your way by! Beware the damn near Rentscler though!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 - Put in at the old Wayne Madison Bridge, paddle up river to the island and make landing, and then float back down with the current. This is the approach used to place the cache. At the time of placement, GMR was running at about 3000 CFS on the &lt;a href="http://waterdata.usgs.gov/usa/nwis/uv?03272100" target="mtgauge"&gt;Middletown Gauge&lt;/a&gt;, which is a bit higher than normal, but if you're careful about where you are in the stream, you can make the island without a lot of effort.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;A couple warnings:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;- Again, &lt;b&gt;Do not try to swim to this!&lt;/b&gt; The currents are interesting for paddle craft. They are down right dangerous for a swimmer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;- If you come down from up river &lt;b&gt;DO NOT take the smaller southerly channel around the island!&lt;/b&gt; there is a &lt;b&gt;DANGEROUS strainer&lt;/b&gt; just below the upriver split.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;- Also beware the current along the north bank and the man-made erosion control. As it passes the rocks, it creates an intense and unstable eddy which will have your boat doing unusual things if you aren't paying attention!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for visiting our cache! Please be aware, this area does flood. The container is sturdy and cabled to it's hiding spot. If you visit after flooding though, the cache may be out in the open, so please re-hide it and cover it with debris.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Because of the possibility that the container may be in flood waters at some point, please use extra care to keep the log dry and sealed in it's bag and the container lid tightly closed!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lastly, based on trash and footprints, there is at least a little muggle activity on the island, probably for fishing, so please use stealth as appropriate. A little CITO wouldn't be a bad thing either. Based on other footprints, wildlife also visits, so be sure to take along your camera! We spotted a massive blue heron while we were placing the cache.&lt;/p&gt;

</groundspeak:long_description><groundspeak:encoded_hints>Tethered to the base of a V-shaped growth of trees.</groundspeak:encoded_hints><groundspeak:logs><groundspeak:log id="1020453381"><groundspeak:date>2021-05-22T13:16:47</groundspeak:date><groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type><groundspeak:finder id="25762707">HopkinsFamily12</groundspeak:finder><groundspeak:text encoded="false">Fun find </groundspeak:text></groundspeak:log><groundspeak:log id="956944412"><groundspeak:date>2020-08-21T19:57:12</groundspeak:date><groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type><groundspeak:finder id="13296704">Ledbetterfam</groundspeak:finder><groundspeak:text encoded="false">Found it!  Great kayak from Rentschler!  Great cache!  Left a surprise!!!</groundspeak:text></groundspeak:log><groundspeak:log id="923229967"><groundspeak:date>2020-04-27T12:00:00</groundspeak:date><groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type><groundspeak:finder id="5810465">Triple_P</groundspeak:finder><groundspeak:text encoded="false">What an adventure this was!  I did quite a bit of extensive research in the days leading up to my attempt to try and find this cache.  I looked at the satellite photos of the area trying to determine the best approach to park and kayak out to the island.  My first plan was to park under the overpass at the intersection of Canal and River Ridge Road.  In fact, that's the area I put in my GPS as I was driving towards the drop zone.  Fun fact, there's some signs that say "Authorized vehicles only" Metroparks.  If that isn't scary enough, there's a no trespassing sign up high on the concrete wall of the overpass.  It's kind of hidden in the ivy or other green stuff hanging from the wall.  The distance is .22 miles to the island from this location.   I decided to keep the kayak on my car and see what kind of launch spot was available to me.  I'd have to go down a steep hill with rocks, but it was a clear shot right to the river.  

I decided I should check out the otherside where the suggested parking area [providing by the cache owner] is.  When you arrive there, you're greeted by a guardrail and then a concrete barrier.  Unlike the other location, there's no signs that say private property or no trespassing.  From that parking spot down to the river is about 400 ft.  Now, that 400 ft includes a wall walked path, then a muddy hill with pebbles and rocks coming out of it.  Keep in mind, if you pick this route, you'll have to come back up this way, if you decide to portage back where you started.  After you get down the muddy hill with rocks, you're greeted by a small boulder field that you have to navigate to get down to level ground.  This is all before you even reach the river.  Once you traverse the boulder field, you'll be on flat ground and have to do a little bit of bushwacking to reach the river.  The total distance from the parking area out to the island is .32 miles.  

I decided I should call a friend of mine and consulate with him.  He's 1/2 of my go to people when I need advice on how to approach things from a legal perspective.  That conversation was about 18 minutes.  When it was over, I decided I should attempt this one from the provided parking.  If I was caught in the act of trespassing or stopped by a local near by, I'd have more of a say than if I was caught in the act coming from the closer parking on the otherside of the river. 

Unloaded the kayak, traversed the muddy hill, found my footing on past the boulder field and finally was ready to put the kayak in the water.  This was no easy task, but I managed and wasn't stopped by anyone or even questioned by anyone.  I'll be honest, I haven't had much luck with kayak caches that haven't been found recently.  My hopes were low in that I might not be able to find this one.  My friend pointed out that a TB might be in the container, and that I should grab it, if I find the geocache.  

The weather today was amazing.  Low 60's a slight wind coming at me and a 0% chance of rain.  I was paddling up stream on my out to the island.  Some of the currents were tougher than others, but I have enough experience that I was able to make it through any hard spots without any problems.  Once I made it to land, I was about 130 ft away from the drop zone.  I found a nice place to dock and off I went to the hiding location.  My eyes immediately spotted your larger than life pipe bomb tethered to the tree.  If someone wants to take this thing, they're going to need some kind of wire cutters.  I don't think this one is going anywhere anytime soon.  

I couldn't get the lid open. I used a hard rock near by and bashed the screw loose.  This popped the lid of the container right open.  I reached in and pulled out the TB and logbook.  The logbook is in bad condition.  A new one should be put out here when possible.  I took the TB and left a $20 bill that I found on the ground earlier in the day.  

Here's some 2020 love - 1,331 days  or 3 years 7 months 23 days lonely.  </groundspeak:text></groundspeak:log><groundspeak:log id="634966511"><groundspeak:date>2016-09-05T12:00:00</groundspeak:date><groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type><groundspeak:finder id="17629000">TeamPikachuLibre</groundspeak:finder><groundspeak:text encoded="false">Doc's caches, 1 and 2, are top notch.  Put in at Rentschler Forest and paddled the extra distance, as we felt like really earning this one.  We also did some serious bushwackin to get to the cache and felt obligated to leave a TB, whose goal included bushwacking.  By far, the most interesting swag we have ever come across.  Unfortunately, the cache was damp.  We drained the water and sealed up the baggies best we could.  Thanks for the awesome cache.</groundspeak:text></groundspeak:log><groundspeak:log id="520660523"><groundspeak:date>2015-07-16T12:00:00</groundspeak:date><groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type><groundspeak:finder id="13416168">postalblonde1974</groundspeak:finder><groundspeak:text encoded="false">Was quite the adventure considering the fact I didn't know what it was at first. My 1st one. Great location. Looking forward to finding more.</groundspeak:text></groundspeak:log></groundspeak:logs></groundspeak:cache></wpt><wpt lat="39.43046666666667" lon="-84.47735"><time>2014-07-09T19:22:33</time><name>DA55YCE</name><cmt>Parking area at the dead-end of Old Wayne Madison Rd.</cmt><desc>Old Wayne Madison Bridge</desc><url>https://www.geocaching.com/seek/wpt.aspx?WID=d64d5d41-fbcf-472e-8a98-f0de746099f8</url><urlname>Old Wayne Madison Bridge</urlname><sym>Parking Area</sym><type>Waypoint|Parking Area</type></wpt><wpt lat="39.431666666666665" lon="-84.46866666666666"><time>2014-07-09T19:23:58</time><name>DB55YCE</name><cmt>Dangerous strainer on southerly channel around island.</cmt><desc>Dangerous Strainer</desc><url>https://www.geocaching.com/seek/wpt.aspx?WID=a2d55fe4-9816-4a13-b506-21f60ec5c913</url><urlname>Dangerous Strainer</urlname><sym>Reference Point</sym><type>Waypoint|Reference Point</type></wpt><wpt lat="39.42953333333333" lon="-84.47711666666666"><time>2014-07-09T19:25:43</time><name>DC55YCE</name><cmt>Possible launching point</cmt><desc>Possible Launch Point</desc><url>https://www.geocaching.com/seek/wpt.aspx?WID=3cafcbc8-e562-42dd-a51a-f36a9987117b</url><urlname>Possible Launch Point</urlname><sym>Trailhead</sym><type>Waypoint|Trailhead</type></wpt></gpx>